## Brand guidance

Apply the [brand colour rules](brand-colour.html), [brand typography rules](brand-typography.html), [data communication rules](brand-data.html) and [illustration rules](illustration.html). These adapt the selected GOV.UK Brand sections to civica.uk. The existing token, chart and supplied-artwork specifications remain their implementation sources.

## Writing guide

Start with the [writing guide](../content/index.html), then choose the chapter for the task. The guide combines UK public service content practice with household-specific rules: distinguish a command from its result, explain missing readings and preserve native translations and device names.

The [work-package checklist](../community/guidance-work-package.html) records source coverage and review loops. The [design-system audit](../community/design-system-audit.html) uses the finished rules to identify changes still needed.

## Use roles before values

Choose a role such as primary text, canvas, focus, error or selected control. Resolve the role through the shared theme tokens. Avoid adding a hex value to one component merely because it looks close to the chosen palette.

The canonical palette data is `design-system/palettes.json` in the repository. Generated theme data and YAML must be reproducible from that source. Documentation must not become a competing source of colour truth.

This follows GOV.UK's distinction between [functional colours and colour palettes](https://design-system.service.gov.uk/styles/colour/) while adding the civica.uk brand choices.
